The '''Demographics of Guajicutea''' describe a densely populated, highly urbanized island society whose population has shifted from a small, rural, Guaji-majority island community into a large, service-oriented, multi-ethnic state. In 2025, Guajicutea's population was about 2.84 million, or roughly 866 people per square kilometre. Population growth has slowed sharply since the 2010s as birthrates fell below replacement, so recent growth depends increasingly on immigration, especially foreign workers and retirees.

Guajicutea remains majority Guaji, but it also has large Bayani, Kaftian, and Sartan-Guaji mixed communities, plus smaller Zong and other immigrant populations. The country is broadly bilingual in Guaji and Sartan, while religion is dominated by [[Christianity in Guajicutea|Petran Catholicism]], alongside [[Islam in Guajicutea|Sunni Bayani Muslim]] communities, [[Warí faith in Guajicutea|traditional Warí faith]], [[Warí Revival in Guajicutea|a younger urban Guaji revival]], and a small [[Tianism in Guajicutea|Tianist]] minority.

== Population history ==

The population of Guajicutea has risen unevenly over time. It remained small through antiquity, expanded slowly through the first millennium, and then grew rapidly in the modern period under trade, reconstruction, immigration, and urbanization.

