The modern population boom began after the Third Republic's reconstruction period. The 1950 census recorded 490,000 residents, and the island then grew quickly through the baby-boom era, rural-to-urban migration, immigration, and state-led reconstruction. Growth accelerated again in the 1950s and 1960s, slowed during the late twentieth-century crisis years, and then returned to moderate service-led expansion after 2000.

