The Sartan category is broader than a simple ethnic label. It includes long-settled Sartan families, mixed households, and urban residents who identify with Sartan language and civic culture. The "other" category is similarly mixed and can include people from [[Tapuya]], [[Volisania]], [[Balboa]], and other regional or overseas communities.

== Language ==

Guajicutea is officially bilingual in Guaji and Sartan. Since the 1947 constitution, both languages have been taught in schools and used in public life, and neither is formally treated as a national language. In practice, Guaji remains strongest in rural districts, older households, fishing communities, and many inland towns, while Sartan dominates much of urban commerce, government, journalism, and higher administration.

Volisanian is also widely understood, especially in tourism, hospitality, shipping, and other service work, because many visitors and investors come from [[Volisania]], [[Balboa]], and [[Tapuya]], and because [[Baliscano language|Baliscano]] is common in nearby [[Balisca]].

Most urban residents are bilingual, and many younger people move easily between Guaji and Sartan, with Volisanian often added for work. The 2010s and 2020s also strengthened the role of Sartan in tourism and business, while Guaji retained strong symbolic value in family life, folk practice, and cultural politics.

Minority languages survive in some immigrant communities, especially in older Bayani neighborhoods and among recent regional migrants, but language shift toward Guaji, Sartan, and Volisanian is strong.

== Religion ==

Guajicutea's religious life is mixed and layered. Roughly two-thirds of residents are [[Christianity in Guajicutea|Petran Catholic]], making it the largest faith by far. Bayani communities account for most of the island's [[Sunni Islam|Sunni Muslims]], so [[Sunni Islam]] is the largest minority religion. [[Three Powers faith|Traditional Guaji beliefs]] remain active in rural districts and in family ritual, while [[Three Powers Revival|a revived Guaji system]] has become more visible among younger urban residents. [[Tianism]], mostly associated with Zong-descended communities, makes up about 1% of the population. The remainder is largely secular or unaffiliated.

