== Age structure and fertility ==

Birthrates fell below replacement during the 2010s and remained low into the 2020s. That changed the shape of the population. Guajicutea no longer depends on a large stream of native-born children for growth, and its age profile has shifted toward older working-age adults and retirees. In 2025, the population was still relatively young by some island standards, but the movement toward aging was clear: roughly 18% of residents were under 15, about 65% were of working age, and around 17% were 65 or older.

== Urbanization ==

Guajicutea is one of the most urbanized societies in the region. By 2025, about 84% of residents lived in urban areas. The island's growth has concentrated in the capital corridor centered on [[Ganan]] and [[Waribá]], the northern coast, and resort-linked districts.

The main pattern is a three-way split: the capital as the administrative center, Waribá as the port and commercial hub, and smaller coastal and inland towns tied to tourism, fisheries, and services. Rural settlement remains important, but fewer people now depend exclusively on agriculture.

== Migration ==

Migration has been one of the biggest demographic forces in modern Guajicutea. In the mid-twentieth century, the island received large numbers of migrants tied to reconstruction, tourism, and industrial expansion from [[Capuyaquiran]] and [[Abyala]]. Later, the state also drew workers from the wider region and from communities displaced by conflict elsewhere.

Most immigration still comes from the neighboring Capuyaquiran islands. In the 2025 census, the largest island-born communities were counted as follows:

