The digital nomad visa helped formalize a small but visible class of long-stay remote workers in the service economy.

Migration now serves two purposes: filling labour shortages in tourism, construction, and care work, and helping the state maintain growth in a low-fertility society.

== Household patterns ==

Guajicutean household size has fallen over time. Large multi-generational households were common in the reconstruction era, but smaller households are now more typical in urban areas. Rising housing costs, delayed marriage, lower fertility, and more elderly one- and two-person households have all contributed to the change.

At the same time, extended-family ties remain socially important. Adult children often live close to parents even when not in the same home, and kinship networks still play a large role in childcare, migration support, and access to housing.

== Demographic pressures ==

Several pressures now shape Guajicutea's population outlook. The first is aging: the large baby-boom cohort is moving into retirement, increasing demand for healthcare and old-age support. The second is housing: foreign demand, especially from Sartan retirees and investors, pushed urban prices upward. The third is migration: the country increasingly relies on imported labour to sustain growth in tourism and services.

These pressures mean that modern demographic policy is no longer about rapid expansion. It is about managing slower growth, keeping housing affordable, and making room for an older society without weakening the labour market.

